  'home.numbers_title': 'At a glance',
  'home.numbers_lead': 'Release 2.0 model values for a favourable scenario (S1: dark mare, Türkiye spring evening), conditional on the spacecraft reaching its final trajectory and on the stated assumptions.',
  'stat.peak_label': 'Model peak brightness of the flash', 'stat.peak_sub': 'median, source-equivalent at Earth distance; model chance of noticing it with the naked eye ≈ {p}',
  'stat.net_label': 'Model chance a global network records it (S1)', 'stat.net_sub': 'Türkiye only: {a} · public network: {c}',
  'stat.light_label': 'Light travel time from the Moon', 'stat.light_sub': '≈ 380,000 km away',
  'stat.crater_label': 'Expected crater diameter', 'stat.crater_sub': 'a wide range set by the oblique-impact rule; could be imaged within days to months if orbiters are operating',
  'home.see_title': 'What will the public be able to see?',
  'home.see_lead': 'The flash is expected to be brief and faint. What the public should be promised is not a flash seen by eye, but the realistic experience below.',
  'home.see1_t': 'Telemetry and descent images', 'home.see1_d': 'The spacecraft’s last data and camera frames, live, delayed by the light time (≈1.3 s) and the broadcast chain.',
  'home.see2_t': 'Telescope feed', 'home.see2_d': 'A telescope view of the Moon’s dark side with the predicted point marked. The flash is only identifiable if it is bright.',
  'home.see3_t': 'Replay within an hour', 'home.see3_d': 'If professional fast cameras catch the flash, a processed image can be shared quickly; confirmation needs two independent stations.',
  'home.see4_t': 'The crater, weeks later', 'home.see4_d': 'If orbiters such as LRO are still operating, the new crater can be photographed and compared with earlier images.',
  'home.why_title': 'Why is it so hard?',
  'home.why_p1': 'Only a tiny fraction of the impact energy turns into light. For a spacecraft hitting the Moon at about 1.7 km/s, no calibrated measurement of that fraction exists, so the predictions span a wide range.',
  'home.why_p2': 'The flash is expected to be brief, with an uncertain duration (about 0.1–2 s in the model), and it happens near the bright part of the crescent Moon. Under these assumptions, naked-eye or binocular detection is very unlikely, and a phone on its own is unlikely to record it.',
  'home.why_p3': 'The strongest channels are fast visible cameras and near-infrared imaging on 2–4 m telescopes; the infrared advantage depends on the flash temperature and the exposure time. In Türkiye, DAG and TUG could join NELIOTA in Greece in a confirmation chain.',
  'home.go_title': 'More',
  'home.go_explore_t': 'Explore', 'home.go_explore_d': '11 hypothetical points on the Moon, visibility from Earth and detection odds by network.',
  'home.go_see_t': 'What could I see?', 'home.go_see_d': 'What is possible with eyes, binoculars, phones and telescopes, and whether the Moon is up where you are.',
  'home.go_live_t': 'Live', 'home.go_live_d': 'A countdown, watch events and stream links once a date is announced.',
