  'sci.kicker': 'Science & data', 'sci.title': 'How was the study done?',
  'sci.lead': 'This site uses the results of an open, reproducible study of the observability of AYAP-1’s lunar impact. All code, configuration and result files are in the GitHub repository.',
  'sci.summary_title': 'Summary',
  'sci.summary_p1': 'The study scans the whole lunar surface hour by hour from 1 May 2027 to 1 March 2029 to find when an impact point is Earth-facing, dark and observable from 35 candidate observatories, then finds when the spacecraft passes over each point for 36 polar orbit planes and eight positions along the orbit. This is a geometric opportunity analysis; burn planning and navigation are not included.',
  'sci.summary_p2': 'The flash is modelled as a cooling blackbody; temperature and luminous efficiency are left within wide ranges, and the total radiated energy may not exceed 10% of the impact energy. The ejecta plume follows the Housen and Holsapple (2011) scaling law, only within its valid range, with particle trajectories traced. Detection uses the light each exposure actually collects from the flash light curve; the detection method is tested by injecting flashes into synthetic video.',
  'sci.summary_p3': 'Finally, event-level simulations are run for each scenario with a shared impact time and location, correlated weather, readiness, exposure timing and calibration uncertainty; the three network strategies are compared on the same simulated events. The results recommend a public programme built on telemetry, a rapid professional replay and a later crater image, rather than a flash promised to the eye. The probabilities are conditional on the stated assumptions; they are not calibrated forecasts.',
  'sci.findings_title': 'Main findings',
  'sci.f1': 'How bright the flash is depends on the fraction of the energy that becomes light, which is unknown. Laboratory trends extended to this speed imply a far smaller fraction, in which case no instrument would see the flash. Under the wide assumption the median is ≈{med} mag (source-equivalent at Earth distance).',
  'sci.f2': 'In the favourable scenario (S1) the model chance of at least one recording is {a} for Türkiye alone and {b} for a global network; {two} for recordings at two separate sites (conditional on the spacecraft reaching that terminal state).',
  'sci.f3': 'Seeing it with the naked eye, binoculars or a phone on its own is very unlikely; the conditional visual-threshold model gives ≈{eye} for noticing it by eye through a 20 cm telescope.',
  'sci.f4': 'In the overflight analysis, which follows the position along the orbit, the chance that a 30-day terminal window contains at least one suitable pass on a Turkish evening is ≈{a} on average for a random plane and phase, and ≈{b} for a pass with ≥3 observatories at any hour. These are geometric opportunities, not a mission plan.',
  'sci.f5': 'A sunlit ejecta plume needs a point just beyond the sunrise terminator. In these scenarios the ejecta either lie outside the validity range of the scaling law or are far too faint against the background (best case SNR ≈{snr}).',
  'sci.f6': 'The crater diameter is ≈{min}–{max} m depending on the oblique-impact rule. If LRO is operating and passes over the site in suitable lighting, the crater could be imaged within days to months; this is not a promise.',
  'sci.methods_title': 'Method steps',
  'm.1_t': 'Geometry', 'm.1_d': 'JPL DE421 ephemeris and a lunar orientation model, compared like-for-like with JPL Horizons tables (angles within ≈0.00001°; the screening geometry is accurate to ≈0.01°).',
  'm.2_t': 'Overflight opportunities', 'm.2_d': 'For a 100 km polar orbit, the times the spacecraft passes over each point, for 36 planes and 8 positions along the orbit, with 0.6° (≈17 m/s) and 2.5° (≈71 m/s) cross-track allowances. Not a burn plan.',
  'm.3_t': 'Impact physics', 'm.3_d': 'Energy, a cooling-blackbody flash, a particle-trajectory ejecta model and crater scaling, compared without tuning with artificial craters imaged by LROC.',
  'm.4_t': 'Detectability', 'm.4_d': 'Signal-to-noise from the light collected in each exposure, earthshine and scattered light, atmospheric extinction and detector saturation; a conditional threshold model for the eye and a broad band for phones.',
  'm.5_t': 'Network simulation', 'm.5_d': 'An event-level Monte Carlo: shared impact time and location, correlated weather, readiness, field coverage and exposure timing; 30,000 events per scenario with uncertainty ranges.',
  'm.6_t': 'Public engagement', 'm.6_d': 'A planned study with event attendees, stream viewers and a comparison group. Its design is to be preregistered in an open registry before the event.',
  'sci.fig_title': 'Figures', 'sci.fig_lead': 'Selected figures from the analysis (labelled in English). Click to enlarge.',
  'sci.data_title': 'Data and code',
  'sci.data_p': 'Scenario cards (JSON), tables (CSV), figures and all the Python code are in the repository. This site’s data files are generated from the same results by <code>scripts/make_site_data.py</code>.',
  'sci.repro_title': 'Reproduce it on your own computer',
  'sci.repro_p': 'With Python 3.12–3.14 you can set up the environment and re-run every calculation (about an hour).',
  'sci.limits_title': 'Limitations',
  'lim.1': 'The orbit plane, final mass, burn plan, target and time have not been published; the results depend on these assumptions.',
  'lim.2': 'No calibrated luminous efficiency is established for a spacecraft impact at these low speeds; the brightness prediction is the largest source of uncertainty.',
  'lim.3': 'Real terrain data (LOLA) could not be used; for points near the limb the terrain effect is only illustrated with a synthetic-terrain experiment.',
  'lim.4': 'The observatories are candidates; none has agreed to take part. Weather probabilities are climatological averages.',
  'lim.5': 'The phone and eye models are simplified; rehearsals with real equipment are needed.',
  'lim.6': 'An independent scientific review (October 2026) found errors in the ejecta-plume brightness, in how cameras and confirmations were simulated, and in some labels. Version 2.0 corrects them; a point-by-point response is in the repository (docs/AUDIT_RESPONSE.md).',
  'sci.cite_title': 'Citation', 'sci.manuscript': 'Paper',

